-- title:
--   Diamond action on first components of specialised norm-free classes
-- statement:
--   The statement is a clause in a large assembly describing the specialisation at $p$ of norm-free points of $J_1(Mp)$; all of its data are hypotheses, and the conclusion concerns the diamond operators $\langle u\rangle$ with $u\equiv 1$ modulo $M$.
--
--   **Arithmetic setting.** A prime $p$, a nonzero natural number $M$ with $5\le M$ (`hM`) and $p\nmid M$ (`hpM`); a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, together with a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta\in L$ (`hζ`); an intermediate field $K$ of $L\subseteq L(\!(q)\!)$ which by `hK` equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; a discrete valuation domain $A$ with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with the tower $A\subseteq L\subseteq K$; and an element $j\in K$, nonzero, whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the $j$-function (`hj`).
--
--   The curve over $A$ is [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) with structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), glued from the spectra of the $A$-subalgebras `chartAlgFin` and `chartAlgInf` of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, respectively over $A[j^{-1}]$; properness of `modelTo` is assumed, as are $A$- and $L$-algebra structures on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ forming a tower.
--
--   **The place above $p$.** A valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `hPl : Pl.LiesOverPrime p`, that is, $p$ is a nonunit of $Pl$; its residue field $\kappa:=$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField ↥Pl` is assumed algebraically closed of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra. A ring map $\rho : A \to Pl$ is given with `hρ` (followed by $Pl\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ it is the structure map $A\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$) and `hAlgκ` (the structure map $A\to\kappa$ is $\rho$ followed by the residue map).
--
--   **The two components of the special fibre.** Schemes $C_1,C_2$ over $\kappa$ via $c_1,c_2$, each proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, together with closed immersions $i_1,i_2$ of $C_1,C_2$ into the $\kappa$-fibre of the two-chart model, over $\kappa$. By `hcover` every point of that fibre lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; by `hred` the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced, and by `hn`, `hn0` its cardinality is $n>0$. There are a section $\varepsilon$ of the two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec}A$ and sections $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2$ of $c_1,c_2$, with `hε₁` saying that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $\kappa$, and `hεC₂` saying that the reduced section misses the image of $i_2$.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** A relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation $D$ for the two-chart model (a scheme `D.P` over $\operatorname{Spec}A$ with a zero section), with `hrep` a witness that $D$ represents the functor of $\varepsilon$-rigidified line bundles which are fibrewise algebraically trivial (`algEquivZeroCut`), and `hsm`, `hsep` asserting that `D.toBase` is smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for the base change to $\kappa$, and `hPk` compares its Poincaré bundle with the base change of the Poincaré bundle of $D$; `hDL` and `hPL` are the analogues over $L$. Designations $D_1,D_2$ for $c_1,c_2$ with representability `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` are given, and a morphism $\nu_2$ from the $\kappa$-base change of $D$ to $D_2$ over $\kappa$, characterised by `hν₂`: for every $T\to\operatorname{Spec}\kappa$ and every point $a$ of the $\kappa$-base change over $T$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` of the pullback along `curveChange i₂` of `(hreps.poincare.pullbackAlong a).L`; thus $\nu_2$ is restriction of line bundles to $C_2$. Further geometric hypotheses over $L$: `hsmL` and `hgiL` (the $L$-base change of the two-chart model is smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral) and `hprL`, `hgcL` (the $L$-fibre of `D.toBase` is proper and geometrically connected).
--
--   **The geometric fibre and its $q$-expansion pinning.** A curve model $M_\eta$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (a smooth proper integral curve with a fixed isomorphism of its function field with that field and a bijection between closed points and places), an isomorphism $e_\eta$ of `Mη.C` with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-fibre of the two-chart model compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`), the nonemptiness instance for the preimage of the finite chart, and `hMηpin`, which says that for every $a$ in `chartAlgFin A K j` the germ of $a$ at the generic point, read in `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` through `Mη.ffEquiv.symm`, has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image under $L\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the expansion of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` states that for $g\in\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing $L$ pointwise and for $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x,x'$ of `Mη.C` with $x'$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$ after transport by $e_\eta$ and the first projection, the place attached to $x'$ is the translate of the place attached to $x$ by [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) applied to $g$.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi dictionary.** A bijection `gpts` between $J_1(Mp):=$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`, and the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of `D.toBase`; a morphism `ajL` over the $L$-base change, a comparison morphism `kL` between the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$- and $L$-fibres, a morphism `ajbar` from `Mη.C` to `D.P`, and a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point `εbar` of `Mη.C`. The hypotheses `hajLε` (the $L$-section is sent to the zero section), `hajL` (for every field $K'$ over $L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajL` is the line bundle of the divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the section, i.e. the class $[x]-[\varepsilon]$), `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj` and `hpts_aj` (for $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$ and $s$ with $s$ the given section there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $\mathrm{single}(x)-\mathrm{single}(s)$ whose class corresponds under `gpts` to the point $x$ followed by `ajbar`) fix this dictionary as the Abel–Jacobi map.
--
--   **Hecke, diamond and Galois structure.** A family $\varphi$ of endomorphisms of `D.toBase` indexed by [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) $=\mathbb{Z}[X_\ell,X_d]$, and a family $\tau$ of automorphisms of `D.P` semilinear over the Galois action of $L\!\simeq_{\mathbb{Q}}\!L$ on $A$ (with `MulSemiringAction (L ≃ₐ[ℚ] L) A` and `hΓA` making that action compatible with the action on $L$). The hypotheses are: `hφmul` ($\varphi(t)$ is additive for the relative group law attached to `hrep.some`), `hφpts` ($\varphi$ computes the Hecke module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) through `gpts`), `hτ1`, `hτmul` ($\tau$ is multiplicative), `hτφ` ($\tau$ and $\varphi$ commute), `hgadd` (`gpts` is additive), `hτpts` (Galois equivariance of `gpts`: for $\sigma'$ restricting to $s$ on $L$, the point of $\sigma'\cdot x$ is $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma')$ followed by the point of $x$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$), together with `hin : HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)` and `hcomm : HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`, which provide the Hecke correspondences and diamond automorphisms at level $Mp$ and their commutation, and `hεgal`, the Galois equivariance of the section $\varepsilon$ for endomorphisms of the two-chart model induced coefficientwise on the finite chart.
--
--   **Abstract special fibre.** An object $G$ of type [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups `G.J0s`, `G.JI`, `G.JE`, a subgroup `G.torus` and a surjection `G.proj : G.J0s →+ G.JI × G.JE` with kernel `G.torus`. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` of these groups with the $\kappa$-points of the $\kappa$-base change of `D.toBase`, of `D₁.toBase` and of `D₂.toBase`; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say that each bijection is additive at the level of pullbacks of Poincaré bundles (the bundle at a sum is the tensor product of the bundles); `hPTS` says in addition that `pts` is a homomorphism for the relative group law and sends $0$ to the unit; `hproj` identifies `G.proj` with the pair of restriction maps, namely restriction to $C_1$ along `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some` and restriction to $C_2$ along $\nu_2$. An operator datum `O : ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreOpsV3 G` (Hecke operators on the three groups, the diamond families `diamondP`, `diamondN`, the involution `w`, the inertia action, `verI`, `frobE`, `diamondNI`, with their commutation relations and the triviality of `diamondP` and `inertia` on the torus) is given, and `hO` has four clauses identifying, through `pts` and the first projection: `O.hecke ℓ` with $\varphi(\mathtt{heckeGenOne}\,\ell)$ for primes $\ell$; `O.diamondP b` with $\varphi(\mathtt{diamondGen}\,d)$ for $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d\equiv 1 \bmod M$ and $d\equiv b \bmod p$; `O.diamondN d` with $\varphi(\mathtt{diamondGen}\,d)$ for $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d\equiv1\bmod p$; and `O.inertia b` with $\tau(s)$ for $s$ with $s\zeta=\zeta^{b}$.
--
--   **Integral points and the norm-free part.** The hypothesis `hF3` asserts: for every subgroup $I$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ contained in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, acting trivially on all $p$-th roots of unity in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and of finite index in that inertia subgroup, writing $\mathcal{O}_I := Pl \cap \overline{\mathbb{Q}}^{\,I}$, there exist a ring map $\rho_I : A \to \mathcal{O}_I$ compatible with $A\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that, with $\mathrm{dom}$ the set of $x\in J_1(Mp)$ whose $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$: $I$ fixes every element of $\mathrm{dom}$; two $\mathcal{O}_I$-points with equal $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points coincide; $\mathrm{dom}$ contains $0$ and is closed under differences; for every $n>0$ prime to $p$, an $n$-torsion $\mathcal{O}_I$-point with trivial reduction to $\kappa$ is trivial and every $n$-torsion $\kappa$-point lifts to an $n$-torsion $\mathcal{O}_I$-point; and $\mathrm{dom}$ is stable under every Frobenius element at $Pl$ normalising $I$. A scheme $\mathcal{A}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}A$ with a morphism $\iota$ into `D.P` over $A$ is given, and `h𝒜` states: $\iota$ is a closed immersion; $\mathcal{A}$ is proper and smooth over $A$ with connected geometric fibres; for every base the $T$-points of $\mathcal{A}$ contain the unit and are closed under the multiplication and inversion of the relative group law; an element $x\in J_1(Mp)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) (the image of the endomorphism `normFreeEnd` attached to `normFreeRepsAt (M * p) p`) if and only if the point `gpts x` factors through $\mathcal{A}$; and $\mathcal{A}$ is stable under every $\varphi(t)$. The hypothesis `hF10` states that for every commutative $\kappa$-algebra $T'$ and every $T'$-point $v$ of the $\kappa$-base change of `D.toBase` which lifts to $\mathcal{A}$, the composition of $v$ with $\varphi\bigl(\sum_{b\in(\mathbb{Z}/p)^\times}\mathtt{diamondGen}\,d_b\bigr)$, where $d_b$ is the natural representative of the element of $\mathbb{Z}/p M$ with components $(b,1)$ under the Chinese remainder isomorphism, is the unit point.
--
--   **Igusa models and glued Picard group.** An integral weight-one form $w$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$ over $\kappa$ (a weight-one modular form with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction `intSeriesC κ w.series` is nonzero), whence the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), obtained by adjoining to the mod-$p$ $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(M)$ the inverse of that reduction. Curve models `Mdl₁`, `Mdl₂` over $\kappa$ of this field together with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathtt{Mdl₁.C}\cong C_1$, $e_2 : \mathtt{Mdl₂.C}\cong C_2$ over $\kappa$ (`he₁`, `he₂`); the nonemptiness instance `hne₁` and the hypothesis `hgauss₁`, which pins $e_1$ by $q$-expansions: if $a$ lies in the finite chart algebra and power series $x,y$ over $A$ satisfy $\bar y\ne0$ in $\kappa$ and $a\cdot y = x$ in $L(\!(q)\!)$, then the germ of $a$ on $C_1$, read in the Igusa function field, has Laurent expansion $\bar x/\bar y$. A finite set `nodesIg` of pairs of places of the Igusa function field, an isomorphism $\Psi$ of `G.J0s` with [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0 κ (Igusa) nodesIg`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L201) (admissible gluing data modulo glued principal data), and isomorphisms $\theta_1,\theta_2$ of `G.JI`, `G.JE` with $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa function field. The hypothesis `hF4c` has seven clauses: `nodesIg` consists exactly of the pairs of places coming from $\kappa$-points of the intersection of $i_1$ and $i_2$; its cardinality is $n$; both projections are injective on it; `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair nodesIg (Ψ x) = (θ₁ (G.proj x).1, θ₂ (G.proj x).2)`; the image of `G.torus` under $\Psi$ is the range of `GluedPic0.nodeUnit nodesIg`; and $\theta_1,\theta_2$ are computed by the divisor classes $[x]-[\varepsilon_1]$, respectively $[x]-[\varepsilon_2]$. Finally a semilinear automorphism `frobT` of the Igusa function field over $\kappa$ with `hfrobT` saying that it raises every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power.
--
--   **The abstract specialisation family.** For each subgroup $I$ as in `hF3` (contained in the inertia subgroup at $Pl$, trivial on $p$-th roots of unity, of finite index), a subgroup `dom𝒟 I …` of $J_1(Mp)$ and an additive map `sp𝒟 I …` from it to `GluedPic0 κ (Igusa) nodesIg` are given. The interface `hIFm` states that $y$ lies in `dom𝒟 I …` exactly when the point `gpts y` factors through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$ along the map chosen by `hF3`, and that `sp𝒟 I … ⟨y, hy⟩` equals $\Psi$ of the $\kappa$-point obtained by reducing such a factorisation. The hypothesis `hlev` has two clauses: for $J\le I$ both admissible, an $\mathcal{O}_I$-point of $D$ representing $y$ pushes forward to an $\mathcal{O}_J$-point representing $y$ with the same reduction to $\kappa$; and consequently `dom𝒟 I … ⊆ dom𝒟 J …` with `sp𝒟 J …` agreeing with `sp𝒟 I …` on it.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every natural number $u$ with $u \equiv 1 \pmod{(Mp)/p}$ there is an additive endomorphism $\theta$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0\bigl(\kappa, \mathtt{igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w}\bigr)$ such that for all subgroups $I$ and $I'$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$, each contained in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, each fixing every $\zeta$ with $\zeta^p=1$, and each of finite index in that inertia subgroup, and for every $y \in$ [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) lying in `dom𝒟 I …` and such that [`ModularCurve.diamondOneBar (M * p) u y`](def/ModularCurve_X1Diamond.html#L98) lies in `dom𝒟 I' …`, the first component of
--   $$\mathtt{GluedPic0.toPic0Pair nodesIg}\bigl(\mathtt{sp𝒟}\,I'\,\ldots\,\langle \mathtt{diamondOneBar } (M p)\, u\, y\rangle\bigr)$$
--   equals $\theta$ applied to the first component of $\mathtt{GluedPic0.toPic0Pair nodesIg}\bigl(\mathtt{sp𝒟}\,I\,\ldots\,\langle y\rangle\bigr)$. Here `diamondOneBar (M * p) u` is the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear endomorphism of $J_1(Mp)$ induced by the diamond automorphism of the function field attached to $u$. The map $\theta$ is quantified before $I$, $I'$ and $y$, so it depends on $u$ only; nothing is asserted about the second component of the pair.
--
--   This is the clause of the norm-free specialisation package for $J_1(Mp)$ which records that the diamond operators $\langle u\rangle$ with $u\equiv 1 \bmod M$ preserve the component of the special fibre carrying the reduction of the cusp and act there through a single additive endomorphism of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa curve, independently of the inertia subgroup used to define the specialisation map. It is used in the assembly [`ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div), which feeds the level-lowering step at $p$.
